For the paper below, fill each field FACTUALLY, in the paper's own terms, briefly (one or two short phrases). Do not classify into categories of your own, do not evaluate or critique: report only what the paper states.
Write "not stated" when the paper does not say. Fields:
- study_type: what kind of paper it is, as the paper itself presents it (e.g. system with evaluation, survey, conceptual framework, benchmark)
- RQ1 task_stage: the security task(s) the system performs, in the paper's words
- RQ1 domain: the deployment setting (e.g. enterprise network, cloud, ICS/OT, IoT, web apps)
- RQ1 orientation: defensive, offensive, or both, as stated
- RQ2 architecture: the agent design: single/multi-agent, roles, planning loop, memory, tools
- RQ2 llm_used: model names and versions
- RQ2 adaptation: prompting, RAG, fine-tuning, RL, etc., as stated
- RQ3 autonomy_level: what the system does without a human, in the paper's words
- RQ3 hitl: where humans approve, review or intervene, as stated
- RQ4 dataset: datasets used
- RQ4 environment: testbed, range, simulator or live environment
- RQ4 metrics: evaluation metrics
- RQ4 baseline_type: what kind of baselines are compared (e.g. rule-based, ML, human, other LLM agents)
- RQ4 headline_result: the main quantitative result, with numbers as reported
- RQ4 compared_to: the named baselines
- RQ4 code_available: yes + URL / no / not stated
- RQ4 data_available: yes + URL / no / not stated
- RQ5 risks_addressed: safety, misuse or reliability risks the paper discusses
- RQ5 mitigations: guardrails or mitigations it proposes or uses
- author_limitations (verbatim): the authors' own limitations statement, verbatim, <=60 words
- author_future_work (verbatim): the authors' future-work statement, verbatim, <=60 words
- evidence (page/section): for the fields above, the section names or numbers where you found them
